{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 26,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.085,
            0.882,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "**Q.8. Twin primes are pairs of primes having a difference of 2. For example, 3 and 5 are twin primes. So are 17 and 19. Find the other twin primes between 1 and 100.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.13,
            0.855,
            0.148
          ],
          "content": "Ans. 3 and 5, 5 and 7, 11 and 13, 17 and 19, 29 and 31, 41 and 43, 59 and 61, 71 and 73."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.157,
            0.648,
            0.176
          ],
          "content": "**Q.9 Identify whether each statement is true or false. Explain.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.184,
            0.607,
            0.203
          ],
          "content": "**a. There is no prime number whose units digit is 4.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.212,
            0.53,
            0.23
          ],
          "content": "**b. A product of primes can also be prime.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.239,
            0.535,
            0.257
          ],
          "content": "**c. Prime numbers do not have any factors.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.266,
            0.555,
            0.284
          ],
          "content": "**d. All even numbers are composite numbers.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.293,
            0.882,
            0.329
          ],
          "content": "**e. 2 is a prime and so is the next number, 3. For every other prime, the next number is composite.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.339,
            0.881,
            0.357
          ],
          "content": "Ans. a. True, unit digit 4 means even number. We know that 2 is the only even prime number."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.366,
            0.651,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": "b. False, product of primes becomes a composite number."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.392,
            0.78,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": "c. False, prime numbers have exactly two factors: 1 and the number itself."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.42,
            0.662,
            0.438
          ],
          "content": "d. False, all even numbers except 2 are composite numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.448,
            0.881,
            0.482
          ],
          "content": "e. True, as every pair, other than (2, 3), contains an even number which is a composite number."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.492,
            0.881,
            0.527
          ],
          "content": "**Q.10. Which of the following numbers is the product of exactly three distinct prime numbers: 45, 60, 91, 105, 330?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.537,
            0.881,
            0.555
          ],
          "content": "Ans. 105 = 3 × 5 × 7 is the only number which is the product of three distinct prime numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.564,
            0.881,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": "**Q.11. How many three-digit prime numbers can you make using each of 2, 4 and 5 once?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.592,
            0.23,
            0.608
          ],
          "content": "Ans. None"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.619,
            0.883,
            0.672
          ],
          "content": "**Q.12. Observe that 3 is a prime number, and 2 × 3 + 1 = 7 is also a prime. Are there other primes for which doubling and adding 1 gives another prime? Find at least five such examples.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.681,
            0.881,
            0.745
          ],
          "content": "Ans. Yes, 11 = 2 × 5 + 1, 47 = 2 × 23 + 1, 83 = 2 × 41 + 1 23 = 2 × 11 +1, 59 = 2 × 29 +1 (Try other possibilities.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.493,
            0.927,
            0.506,
            0.94
          ],
          "content": "5"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
